\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How Small Businesses Can Stay Safe in a Digital World\
\
\
\
Running a small business often feels like a juggling act \'96 you\'92ve got customers to serve and operations to manage. The last thing you need is a cybercriminal (possibly 
\f1\b half a world away
\f0\b0 ) sneaking into your systems. Yet that threat is very real: nearly 
\f1\b 46% of known cyber breaches
\f0\b0 \'a0affect businesses with under 1,000 employees, and 
\f1\b 61% of small companies
\f0\b0 \'a0have been targeted by cyberattacks in recent years . Despite this growing risk, over 
\f1\b 51% of small businesses have no cybersecurity measures
\f0\b0 \'a0in place \'96 many owners assume they\'92re 
\f2\i \'93too small\'94
\f0\i0 \'a0to be attacked . Unfortunately, hackers count on this false sense of security. In fact, even nation-state hackers have exploited small businesses (for example, a 
\f1\b Russian hacking campaign infiltrated hundreds of U.S. home and small business routers
\f0\b0 \'a0to launch larger cyber operations ). The takeaway? 
\f1\b Cybercrime is a growing threat
\f0\b0 \'a0for 
\f1\b businesses of every size
\f0\b0 , and it\'92s time to take it seriously. The good news is you 
\f2\i can
\f0\i0 \'a0fight back. Below, we\'92ll break down the most important steps to keep your small business safe in today\'92s digital world \'96 all with a warm dose of confidence and a cheeky wink where needed.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 1. Guard Every Credit Card Transaction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\
Small businesses live on transactions \'96 from swipes at the register to online checkouts. Keeping those 
\f1\b credit card payments secure
\f0\b0 \'a0is vital for customer trust 
\f2\i and
\f0\i0 \'a0your bottom line. Start by using 
\f1\b reputable, PCI-compliant payment systems
\f0\b0 \'a0(think well-known providers that 
\f1\b encrypt
\f0\b0 \'a0card data and follow industry security standards). Never store sensitive card information on paper or unsecured computers. If you use point-of-sale devices, make sure they\'92re using 
\f1\b EMV chip readers
\f0\b0 \'a0or contactless payments, which are far safer than old magnetic stripe swipes. And beware of skimmers or tampering \'96 regularly inspect card readers for anything suspicious. Shockingly, 
\f1\b 27% of small businesses with no cybersecurity protections still collect customers\'92 credit card info
\f0\b0 \'a0, leaving both the business and customers exposed. Don\'92t be that statistic. Lock down your transaction process so hackers can\'92t skim card numbers or intercept payments. Your customers will thank you \'96 and so will your future self, knowing you\'92ve prevented a nightmare scenario of fraudulent charges or a data breach.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 How Little Fight NYC Can Help:
\f0\i0 \'a0Secure transactions don\'92t have to be complicated. We consult on 
\f1\b POS system setup
\f0\b0 \'a0to ensure you\'92re using up-to-date, secure payment tech. From choosing a safe payment processor to configuring your in-store Wi-Fi for secure transactions, our team has you covered.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 2. Use Industry-Standard Protection for Websites and POS Systems\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\
If your business has a website or uses a modern POS system (like an iPad at the counter), make sure they\'92re protected by 
\f1\b industry-standard safeguards
\f0\b0 . This means 
\f1\b choosing tools and vendors with built-in security
\f0\b0 \'a0\'96 don\'92t cut corners with outdated software or cheap, unvetted apps. For websites, use trusted e-commerce platforms or plugins that are known for security (and keep them updated, which we\'92ll cover in a bit). For point-of-sale, opt for providers that offer 
\f1\b end-to-end encryption
\f0\b0 \'a0of transactions and robust fraud detection. Ensure your POS software is regularly updated/patched by the vendor to fix any security holes. It\'92s also wise to 
\f1\b enable PCI DSS compliance features
\f0\b0 \'a0(most reputable POS and e-commerce systems will guide you through PCI Security Standards, which are basically the rules for handling credit card data safely). In short, stick with the majors and the specialists \'96 those systems are constantly tested against threats. If navigating all this tech feels overwhelming, remember you don\'92t have to do it alone. 
\f1\b Little Fight NYC
\f0\b0 \'a0can step in as your personal tech translator: we help evaluate and implement secure POS solutions and 
\f1\b develop websites with security best practices
\f0\b0 \'a0baked right in. By using enterprise-level protections scaled for a small biz, you make it 
\f2\i much
\f0\i0 \'a0harder for cybercriminals to find a way in.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 3. Lock Down Devices and Accounts (Local Security)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\
Think about all the devices and accounts that keep your business running: the store laptop, the tablet running your cash register, employee smartphones checking email, your Wi-Fi router, and countless logins for services. Each one is a potential door into your business\'92s data. 
\f1\b Lock them down.
\f0\b0 \'a0Start with your hardware: enable passcodes or biometric locks on every device and turn on automatic locking when idle. Use 
\f1\b MDM (Mobile Device Management)
\f0\b0 \'a0on company phones/tablets \'96 this lets you remotely wipe a device if it\'92s lost or stolen and enforce security policies (like strong passwords and not installing random apps). Little Fight NYC often helps clients set up MDM so they have peace of mind even when devices leave the building. For computers and routers, always change default passwords (no \'93admin/admin\'94 please!) and keep their firmware updated.\
\
Just as critical are your 
\f1\b user accounts
\f0\b0 \'a0for email, banking, social media, your website, etc. Use 
\f1\b unique, strong passwords
\f0\b0 \'a0for each (a password manager can really help generate and remember them). Better yet, enable 
\f1\b multi-factor authentication (MFA)
\f0\b0 \'a0wherever possible \'96 that\'92s the \'93text message code\'94 or authenticator app prompt you get when logging in. Yes, it adds one extra step, but it blocks the vast majority of unauthorized access. Remember, a huge chunk of attacks succeed through stolen or cracked passwords \'96 in fact, about 
\f1\b 80% of hacking incidents involve compromised credentials or passwords 
\f0\b0 . Don\'92t make it easy for the bad guys: if your username and password do leak in a breach, MFA can stop them cold. And if all this sounds like a lot to manage, don\'92t worry. We routinely help small businesses implement 
\f1\b account-level protections
\f0\b0 . Little Fight NYC can assist in setting up password managers for your team and configuring MFA on your critical accounts \'96 keeping the keys to your kingdom firmly in 
\f2\i your
\f0\i0 \'a0hands.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 4. Protect Your People and Personal Devices (Passwords & Phishing)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\
Even the best technology won\'92t help if your team is tricked into letting hackers in. That\'92s why 
\f1\b company and personal device protection
\f0\b0 \'a0is as much about people as machines. Make 
\f1\b cybersecurity training
\f0\b0 \'a0a regular part of your business operations \'96 even if your \'93team\'94 is just you and a couple of employees. Phishing emails are still the #1 way small businesses get breached (96% of phishing attacks arrive by email, and 
\f1\b small firms receive an outsize share of these malicious emails
\f0\b0 \'a0). Teach everyone to be suspicious of unexpected links or attachments, and to double-check unusual requests (like an \'93urgent\'94 wire transfer email that looks like it\'92s from you \'96 classic CEO fraud!). Create a culture where it\'92s okay to 
\f1\b pause and question
\f0\b0 \'a0a strange email or text.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Password management
\f0\b0 \'a0is another human-factor lifesaver. Encourage (or require) use of a reputable password manager so that employees 
\f1\b aren\'92t recycling the same easy password
\f0\b0 \'a0everywhere. Emphasize that personal devices used for work (like that Gmail on a personal phone) need to have strong passwords/PINs and up-to-date security patches too. It only takes one compromised personal phone to potentially expose company email or data. Consider simple policies: for example, require that any device accessing company accounts has a lock screen and isn\'92t rooted/jailbroken. And if something seems phishy, make sure your team knows to report it \'96 better to have a false alarm than a real breach.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 How Little Fight NYC Can Help:
\f0\i0 \'a0We offer 
\f1\b digital best practices training
\f0\b0 \'a0tailored for small businesses. Think short, no-jargon workshops on spotting phishing scams, using password managers, and setting up secure devices. We\'92re a small, scrappy team ourselves, so we get it \'96 the goal is to empower you and your staff to be the first line of defense, without turning everyone into paranoid IT techs. A little knowledge (and a friendly nudge) goes a long way toward keeping your business safe.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 5. Shield Your Website with Updates, SSL, and Login Protection\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\
Your website is your digital storefront \'96 and just like your physical shop, it needs a solid lock on the door. Website attacks on small businesses are rampant (automated bots don\'92t care if you\'92re a mom-and-pop shop or a big corporation). 
\f1\b Protect your website
\f0\b0 \'a0by following these best practices:\
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Enable HTTPS (SSL):
\f0\b0 \'a0Make sure your site has an SSL certificate so that it shows the padlock and 
\f1\b \'93https://\'94
\f0\b0 \'a0in the address bar. This encrypts data between your site and visitors (crucial if you have logins or e-commerce). It also boosts customer confidence \'96 nobody wants to see \'93Not Secure\'94 next to your URL. Most web hosts or providers like Wix, WordPress, or Shopify offer free SSL certificates or easy integration. Use them!\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Keep Software Updated:
\f0\b0 \'a0Whether your site runs on WordPress, another CMS, or a custom platform, 
\f1\b install updates and patches promptly
\f0\b0 . Updates often fix security vulnerabilities hackers are quick to exploit. The same goes for any plugins, themes, or e-commerce modules \'96 outdated plugins are a common entry point for attacks. Set updates to auto-install when possible, or have a schedule to check for them.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Lock Down Logins:
\f0\b0 \'a0Your website\'92s admin login is a favorite target for brute-force attacks (where automated scripts try millions of username/password combos). Strengthen it by using a 
\f1\b strong, unique admin password
\f0\b0 \'a0(no \'93admin123\'94 \uc0\u55357 \u56860 ), and enable two-factor authentication for logins if available. Also consider adding a plugin or service that 
\f1\b limits login attempts
\f0\b0 \'a0or uses a firewall to block suspicious activity. Even a simple CAPTCHA on the login page can stop the dumb bots. And of course, 
\f1\b never reuse credentials
\f0\b0 \'a0from other sites for your admin account.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\
By implementing these measures, you\'92re making your website a hard target. Most opportunistic hackers will give up and move on to easier prey if they can\'92t breach your site quickly. If you\'92re not sure where to start, Little Fight NYC can help here too. We build and 
\f1\b maintain websites with security in mind
\f0\b0 \'a0\'96 from initial SSL setup to ongoing updates and monitoring. Think of it as having an alarm system for your online storefront.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

